<article class="help-article p-4">
    <header class="help-article-header mb-4">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined help-article-icon">table</span>
        <h1 class="help-article-title">Working with Entities</h1>
        <p class="help-article-lead">
            An entity describes one kind of object in your model &mdash; the equivalent of a table in
            a relational database. Every attribute and relationship you add belongs to an entity.
        </p>
    </header>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Add an entity</h2>
    <ol class="help-steps">
        <li>
            <strong>Open the main menu</strong> in the Editor and choose <em>Add Entity</em>.
            A new entity appears in the source list on the left.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Select it</strong> to reveal its properties in the Inspector on the right.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Give it a name.</strong> Use a singular noun, capitalized &mdash; for example
            <em>Customer</em> or <em>Invoice</em>. The name identifies the entity throughout the model
            and in the generated code.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Entity identity</h2>
    <p>The Inspector's <strong>Entity Identity</strong> section controls how the entity behaves:</p>
    <ul class="help-list">
        <li><strong>Parent</strong> &mdash; make this entity inherit from another. Subentities share the parent's attributes and relationships.</li>
        <li><strong>Abstract Entity</strong> &mdash; mark an entity as abstract when it exists only to be inherited from and is never instantiated on its own.</li>
        <li><strong>Class Name</strong> &mdash; controls the generated managed-object class name.</li>
        <li><strong>Type</strong> &mdash; the entity's role in authorization. <em>Authorizable</em> marks the entity that authenticates (the account), <em>Authorizable Role</em> the entity that carries roles, and <em>Authorization</em> the record that links them. These entity-level choices are what make the per-property roles and scopes meaningful &mdash; see <a class="help-link" href="#access-control" data-help-anchor="access-control">Access Control</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Hash Modifier and Renaming ID</strong> &mdash; help version or rename model elements while preserving migration intent.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="help-callout">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined">key</span>
        <div>
            <strong>Uniqueness constraints.</strong>
            Under <em>Class Generation &rarr; Constraints</em> you can require that an attribute's value be
            unique across all records. Add an attribute first &mdash; a constraint references an existing attribute.
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Next steps</h2>
    <p>
        An entity with no fields can't hold data yet. Continue by
        <a class="help-link" href="#attributes" data-help-anchor="attributes">adding attributes</a>,
        then connect entities together by
        <a class="help-link" href="#relationships" data-help-anchor="relationships">adding relationships</a>.
    </p>
</article>
